    "body": "Kurmuk mine site. ( Image courtesy of Allied Gold. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 10, 2026\nAllied Gold (TSX: AAUC; NYSE: AAUC) has poured first gold at its Kurmuk mine in Ethiopia, bringing a major growth project into production as it targets annual output of about 800,000 oz. by 2029.\nThe Toronto-based miner expects Kurmuk to produce 25,000 to 35,000 oz. of gold this year before increasing output to between 240,000 and 270,000 oz. in 2027. Commercial production is targeted by year-end, with the plant expected to reach its designed capacity early next year.\n“The project, operations, and in-country teams have done an outstanding job bringing Kurmuk from a greenfield site to its first gold pour,” Allied’s management and board of directors said in a Nov. 9 statement.\nKurmuk is expected to become Allied’s lowest-cost operation and help lift group production to between 615,000 and 670,000 oz. next year, up 54% at the midpoint from its 2026 forecast of 400,000 to 435,000 ounces. An expansion at Sadiola in Mali underpins further growth towards the 2029 target.\nProduction ramp-up\nKurmuk lies in western Ethiopia, about 750 km west-northwest of the capital, Addis Ababa. Allied expects to finish installing the remaining power infrastructure and mobilizing its full haulage fleet this month. Gold recoveries are expected to reach design levels early in 2027.\nThe company forecasts Kurmuk production of 55,000 to 65,000 oz. in the first quarter of 2027 and about 300,000 oz. in 2028. It expects annual output to average about 300,000 oz. over the first four full years of operations and 240,000 oz. over the mine’s entire life.\nZijin investment\nThe milestone follows the collapse in July of a proposed $5.5-billion (US$4-billion) takeover by China’s Zijin Gold International (HK: 2259). The companies ended the agreement after concluding its closing conditions were unlikely to be satisfied by the deadline or within a reasonable time afterwards. The January offer was $44 a share in cash.\nZijin instead acquired a 9.2% stake through a $416.6-million private placement that closed Aug. 10 . Allied earmarked the proceeds for Kurmuk’s completion and ramp-up, Sadiola’s expansion, production increases in Côte d’Ivoire and exploration across its portfolio.\nAllied is also considering a base dividend starting in 2027 and proposing a normal course issuer bid to repurchase up to 10% of its public float, subject to TSX approval. Those plans form part of a capital allocation strategy that also prioritizes growth investment and maintaining a strong balance sheet.\nAllied shares traded 5.4% weaker at $31.37 in Toronto on Tuesday morning, valuing the company at about $4.4 billion. The stock has traded between $21.94 and $43.77 over the past 12 months.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Africa Canada\nCOMPANY: Allied Gold Zijin Gold International\nRepublish this article",
